      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A memory leak, how frustrating. You're right, it's like a sneaky goblin, hiding in the shadows, eating away at your system's resources. I'm not surprised you're stumped - it's like trying to find a needle in a haystack, but the needle is a memory leak and the haystack is a 512MB system.\n\nTo track down the leak, I'd recommend a systematic approach. First, you'll want to use a memory profiling tool to get an idea of where the memory is being allocated and not freed. There are several options available, such as Valgrind, AddressSanitizer, or Memory Profiler. These tools can help you identify which functions are allocating memory and which are not freeing it.\n\nFor example, Valgrind can be used to run your program under a memory profiler, which will give you a detailed report of all memory allocations and deallocations. You can then use this report to identify which functions are leaking memory.\n\nAnother approach is to use a tool like gprof, which can help you identify which functions are using the most memory. You can then use this information to narrow down your search for the leak.\n\nIn terms of a constrained environment, you can try using a tool like str",
